1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,610
There is a famous saying, often attributed to an Indian field marshal.

2
00:00:05,060 --> 00:00:10,730
If a man says he is not afraid of dying, he is either lying, or he is a Gurkha.

3
00:00:11,180 --> 00:00:17,180
For more than two hundred years, soldiers from the hills of Nepal have fought in Britain's wars.

4
00:00:17,630 --> 00:00:23,840
Thirteen Nepali Gurkhas have won the Victoria Cross, Britain's highest award for bravery.

5
00:00:24,290 --> 00:00:27,850
So who are the Gurkhas, and why did armies fear them?

6
00:00:28,300 --> 00:00:32,710
The name comes from Gorkha, a small hill kingdom in central Nepal.

7
00:00:33,160 --> 00:00:43,380
In our last video, we saw how its king, Prithvi Narayan Shah, united Nepal, and how Kathmandu fell to his army in seventeen sixty-eight.

8
00:00:43,830 --> 00:00:52,340
His soldiers came mostly from Nepal's hill communities: farmers and herders who walked steep mountain paths every single day.

9
00:00:52,790 --> 00:00:55,700
And they carried a curved knife called the khukuri.

10
00:00:56,150 --> 00:01:01,140
In eighteen fourteen, Nepal went to war with the British East India Company.

11
00:01:01,590 --> 00:01:09,480
At a hill fort called Kalunga, also known as Nalapani, a few hundred defenders threw back assault after assault.

12
00:01:09,930 --> 00:01:13,520
The British commander, Major General Gillespie, was killed.

13
00:01:13,970 --> 00:01:18,400
The British officers were deeply impressed by the men who had beaten them back.

14
00:01:18,850 --> 00:01:25,100
Even before the war was over, they began raising units of Gorkha soldiers for the Company.

15
00:01:25,550 --> 00:01:34,980
In eighteen fifteen, two battalions were formed, the Nasiri and the Sirmoor battalions. This is where Gurkha service to Britain begins.

16
00:01:35,430 --> 00:01:41,450
Forty-two years later, in eighteen fifty-seven, rebellion swept across northern India.

17
00:01:41,900 --> 00:01:48,380
The Sirmoor Battalion was sent to the ridge outside Delhi, to hold a building called Hindu Rao's house.

18
00:01:48,830 --> 00:01:51,100
They held it through attack after attack.

19
00:01:51,550 --> 00:01:57,430
Out of about four hundred and ninety men, three hundred and twenty-seven were killed or wounded.

20
00:01:57,880 --> 00:02:05,440
Queen Victoria later gave the battalion a unique honour: a ceremonial staff, known as the Queen's Truncheon.

21
00:02:05,890 --> 00:02:10,539
By now, the Gurkhas had a fearsome reputation, and so did their knife.

22
00:02:10,990 --> 00:02:17,020
The khukuri is about forty to forty-five centimetres long, with a heavy, curved blade.

23
00:02:17,470 --> 00:02:23,510
In Nepal's hills, it's an everyday tool, for cutting wood, clearing paths and chopping food.

24
00:02:23,960 --> 00:02:30,470
Its sheath even holds two small extra blades. One is used to strike sparks and light a fire.

25
00:02:30,920 --> 00:02:35,380
You may have heard that once a khukuri is drawn, it must taste blood.

26
00:02:35,830 --> 00:02:39,430
The Brigade of Gurkhas itself calls that a popular myth.

27
00:02:39,880 --> 00:02:43,760
Still, every Gurkha in the British Army is issued one today.

28
00:02:44,210 --> 00:02:51,150
In the First World War, Gurkhas fought far from home, including in the cold, muddy trenches of France.

29
00:02:51,600 --> 00:02:57,190
Until nineteen eleven, Gurkha soldiers could not even receive the Victoria Cross.

30
00:02:57,640 --> 00:03:04,800
In September nineteen fifteen, near Neuve Chapelle, Rifleman Kulbir Thapa was wounded during an attack.

31
00:03:05,250 --> 00:03:11,510
Behind enemy lines, he found a badly wounded British soldier, and refused to leave him.

32
00:03:11,960 --> 00:03:21,510
He stayed with him all day and all night, then rescued two wounded Gurkhas, and finally carried the British soldier back to safety, under fire.

33
00:03:21,960 --> 00:03:26,310
Kulbir Thapa became the first Gurkha to win the Victoria Cross.

34
00:03:26,760 --> 00:03:31,180
In the Second World War, well over a hundred thousand Gurkhas served.

35
00:03:31,630 --> 00:03:42,530
In May nineteen forty-five, in Burma, Rifleman Lachhiman Gurung was in a forward trench when at least two hundred Japanese soldiers attacked in the night.

36
00:03:42,980 --> 00:03:46,570
A grenade blew off his fingers and shattered his right arm.

37
00:03:47,020 --> 00:03:52,480
For four hours, alone, he kept fighting, firing his rifle with his left hand.

38
00:03:52,930 --> 00:03:57,770
In the morning, thirty-one enemy dead were found in front of his section's position.

39
00:03:58,220 --> 00:04:04,680
Lachhiman Gurung survived, received the Victoria Cross, and lived to the age of ninety-two.

40
00:04:05,130 --> 00:04:07,950
But courage like this came at a terrible price.

41
00:04:08,400 --> 00:04:13,070
More than forty-six thousand Gurkhas have died serving the British Crown.

42
00:04:13,520 --> 00:04:19,050
Most of them were young men from small hill villages, a very long way from home.

43
00:04:19,500 --> 00:04:25,710
When India became independent in nineteen forty-seven, the ten Gurkha regiments were split.

44
00:04:26,160 --> 00:04:30,210
Six joined the new Indian Army, and four stayed with Britain.

45
00:04:30,660 --> 00:04:39,600
That's why today you'll find Gurkhas in both armies, and a Gurkha contingent has served in the Singapore Police since nineteen forty-nine.

46
00:04:40,050 --> 00:04:43,180
Becoming a Gurkha today is incredibly hard.

47
00:04:43,630 --> 00:04:54,150
For the intake that began in January twenty twenty-five, more than sixteen thousand young Nepalis applied, for just two hundred and seventy-four places.

48
00:04:54,600 --> 00:04:58,740
Selection happens in Pokhara, in the shadow of the Himalaya.

49
00:04:59,190 --> 00:05:08,130
One famous test is the doko race: an uphill run with a basket of sand strapped to the back and held by a band across the head.

50
00:05:08,580 --> 00:05:13,380
It's the same way hill porters in Nepal have carried loads for centuries.

51
00:05:13,830 --> 00:05:22,940
In twenty ten, in Helmand, Afghanistan, Acting Sergeant Dipprasad Pun was on guard at a small checkpoint when it was attacked.

52
00:05:23,390 --> 00:05:26,160
He fought off the attack almost single-handed.

53
00:05:26,610 --> 00:05:31,560
At the very end, he even threw his machine-gun tripod at an attacker.

54
00:05:32,010 --> 00:05:37,920
He was awarded the Conspicuous Gallantry Cross, one of Britain's highest awards for bravery.

55
00:05:38,370 --> 00:05:43,640
The motto of the Royal Gurkha Rifles says it all: better to die than be a coward.

56
00:05:44,090 --> 00:05:48,310
And their war cry, Ayo Gorkhali, means: the Gurkhas are here.

57
00:05:48,760 --> 00:05:55,380
More than four thousand Gurkhas serve in the British Army today, over two hundred years after Kalunga.

58
00:05:55,830 --> 00:06:04,830
So why did armies fear them? Not because of a magic knife, but because of toughness, loyalty and courage, tested again and again.

59
00:06:05,280 --> 00:06:09,400
Is there a Gurkha in your family? Tell us their story in the comments.

60
00:06:09,850 --> 00:06:16,570
Next time, we go to the highest place on Earth, where the air itself can kill you: Everest's death zone.

61
00:06:17,020 --> 00:06:19,210
This is Himalaya Unfolded.

